# A cfg condition no build can meet hid 23 tests for 31 days, and their panics were booked as production (2026-10-05)

`crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/auth_method.rs` carried `#[cfg(any())] mod tests` on
line 593. `any()` with no arguments is a well-formed condition that matches nothing, so rustc
drops the module before name resolution: `cargo test` compiled none of it, the `#[ignore]`
ledger had no entry for it, clippy had no warning for it, and `panic-site-census.py` -- whose
cfg evaluator only answers "does this require `test`", and an empty `any()` requires nothing --
counted the 13 `.unwrap()` / `.expect()` calls inside it as production panic sites. Two of the
three things that should have noticed were silent by construction, and the third was wrong in
the direction that made the file look more thoroughly checked than it is.

== 0. What was on disk

    $ git blame -L 593,594 --date=short HEAD -- crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/auth_method.rs
    f4dc6d533 (Chaos 2026-07-26 593) #[cfg(any())]
    f4dc6d533 (Chaos 2026-07-26 594) mod tests {

The module held 23 `#[test]` functions: the classifier matrix for `AuthMethodKind`, the
credential predicate, the env-var precedence rules, the `AuthManager` legacy-token path and the
login-method constructors. Nothing about the file read as uncovered. Twenty-three test functions
sit in it, the census reported the panic sites inside them as production ones, and the last
window in which the module was live, 2026-08-31 to 2026-09-04, is the last evidence anyone had
of it.

== 1. How long, and why three times

The attribute is not a one-off. Walking `git log --follow` over the file and reading the line
above `mod tests {` in each revision gives the whole sequence, with the number of `#[test]`
functions the module held at that revision:

    c68e39f6 2026-07-16  #[cfg(test)]       tests=21
    e9d8fa94 2026-07-26  #[cfg(any())]      tests=21
    f4dc6d53 2026-07-26  #[cfg(any())]      tests=21
    780d1388 2026-08-03  #[cfg(test)]       tests=21
    ed6d5436 2026-08-04  #[cfg(test)]       tests=21
    a5589e95 2026-08-05  #[cfg(test)]       tests=24
    0a9313e7 2026-08-07  #[cfg(any())]      tests=21
    bb7f39d5 2026-08-31  #[cfg(test)]       tests=24
    8dd3a8f9 2026-09-04  #[cfg(any())]      tests=23
    ca69978c 2026-09-23  #[cfg(any())]      tests=23
    228ccf83 2026-10-02  #[cfg(any())]      tests=23
    HEAD    2026-10-05  #[cfg(any())]      tests=23
    (batch) 2026-10-05  #[cfg(test)]       tests=14

Parked, un-parked by an upstream sync, re-parked, un-parked by the next sync, re-parked, and
then left for 31 days. Both re-parks landed on top of the newer version of the module that the
sync had just brought in, so each one also silently discarded upstream's newer tests for the
file. The reason was never "these tests are wrong": the fork pinned authentication to
bring-your-own-key only, several of the upstream assertions stopped describing this fork, and
curating them was deferred. Deferring them by deleting the tests would have been visible; the
attribute made the deferral invisible, including to whoever would later have to pick it up.

== 2. The guard, in both directions

`scripts/ci/check-dead-cfg.py` folds every `cfg` / `cfg_attr` / `cfg!` predicate in `crates/`
and `bin/` to TRUE / FALSE / UNKNOWN, sharing its evaluator with the census through
`scripts/cfg_lib.py`, and reports the FALSE ones. On the tree as it now stands:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-dead-cfg.py; echo "exit=$?"
    2979 files, no cfg predicate that can never hold
    exit=0

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-dead-cfg.py
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    Ran 17 tests in 14.568s

    OK
    exit=0

The negative case is the pristine file, copied into a scratch tree that mirrors the repo layout
one file deep, so the guard has something to find:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-dead-cfg.py --root {SCRATCH}/guardtree; echo "guard exit=$?"
    {SCRATCH}/guardtree/crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/auth_method.rs:593: cfg(any()) can never hold

    1 cfg predicate(s) can never hold, so rustc drops whatever they gate from every build. Test
    modules behind one are compiled by no build: they neither run nor appear in any ledger, and
    the file still reads as covered code. Delete the gated text, or make the condition one a
    build can satisfy.
    guard exit=1

