== 6. The mutation matrix

Every claim in this batch is a claim about a checker, so each was tested by breaking the thing
the checker is supposed to notice. Seven mutants break shipped auth behavior and are expected to
be caught by the revived Rust tests; five break the guard or the census and are expected to be
caught by the Python fixtures; one removes the gate from the container list and is expected to be
caught by the wiring guard.

    $ grep -E '^== M|-> (caught|MISSED)' {SCRATCH}/mutation.log
    == M1 pinned-oidc returns an empty list and no default
    exit 101; names 'every_configuration_advertises_only_api_key': True -> caught
    == M2 unpinned re-advertises cached_token (the upstream regression shape)
    exit 101; names 'legacy_grok_auth_env_is_loaded_but_never_advertised': True -> caught
    == M3 kill switch no longer flips the predicate
    exit 101; names 'disable_api_key_auth_decides_the_predicate_not_the_advertised_list': True -> caught
    == M4 push_interactive_login stops requiring an issuer
    exit 101; names 'push_interactive_login_requires_an_issuer_for_oidc': True -> caught
    == M5 grok_com_auth_method ignores its label
    exit 101; names 'retained_login_methods_still_build': True -> caught
    == M6 legacy GROK_CODE_XAI_API_KEY fallback removed
    exit 101; names 'legacy_env_var_fallback_advertises_xai_api_key': True -> caught
    == M7 cached_token no longer classified as session-based
    exit 101; names 'auth_method_kind_classifier_matrix': True -> caught
    == M8 any() folds TRUE instead of FALSE
    exit 1; names 'FAILED': True -> caught
    == M9 all() folds FALSE instead of TRUE
    exit 1; names 'FAILED': True -> caught
    == M10 guard stops reporting never-true predicates
    exit 1; names 'FAILED': True -> caught
    == M11 census counts never-compiled spans as production again
    exit 1; names 'cfg(any())': True -> caught
    == M12 dead_by_cfg always answers 'compiled'
    exit 1; names 'cfg(any())': True -> caught
    == M13 census re-lists a module declared under a dropped cfg
    exit 1; names 'cfg(any())': True -> caught
    == M14 dead-cfg gate removed from the container gate list
    exit 1; names 'scripts/ci/check-dead-cfg.py': True -> caught

    $ grep -c -- '-> caught' {SCRATCH}/mutation.log
    14
    $ grep -c -- '-> MISSED' {SCRATCH}/mutation.log
    0

    $ tail -1 {SCRATCH}/mutate2.stdout      # the harness's own summary line
    all 14 mutants caught

Two things about the harness were wrong before any mutant was caught, and both are the kind that
produce a green result for no reason. The first version applied no patches at all -- it computed
the mutated text and dropped it -- so all fourteen "caught" verdicts came from running the
unmutated tree, which fails only when a mutant is genuinely load-bearing. The second was M14's
expectation: it waited for the gate label `dead cfg` in the output, while `check-guard-wiring.py`
reports the file it cannot reach, so a mutant the guard did catch was recorded as surviving. Both
were fixed before the run quoted above.


