the module doc no longer claims the fork advertises anything it does not.

== 8. Wiring

A gate nobody runs is how this happened the first two times, so the gate is registered in both
places the wiring guard reads, and the guard confirms it can reach it:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
    check-guard-wiring: OK (85 files in scripts/ci/, 84 reachable, 76 run by scripts/verify-in-docker.sh, 8 recorded CI-only, 1 exempt)
    exit=0

    $ grep -n 'dead cfg' scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
    361:  "dead cfg: python3 scripts/ci/test-check-dead-cfg.py && python3 scripts/ci/check-dead-cfg.py"

    $ grep -n 'check-dead-cfg' .github/workflows/ci.yml
    844:          python3 scripts/ci/test-check-dead-cfg.py
    845:          python3 scripts/ci/check-dead-cfg.py

The shared cfg evaluator went to `scripts/cfg_lib.py`, not `scripts/ci/cfg_lib.py`, for the same
reason the other shared library is there: every file in `scripts/ci/` has to be a gate the runner
can reach, and a library is not one.

The same list runs inside `chaos-verify:local`, build legs included, and the new gate is in it. Its
section, from a `--full` run of this tree:

    $ bash scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --full
    == dead cfg
    test_a_leaf_and_its_own_negation_contradict_only_under_all (__main__.FoldTests.test_a_leaf_and_its_own_negation_contradict_only_under_all) ... ok
    [...]
    Ran 17 tests in 11.616s

    OK
    2979 files, no cfg predicate that can never hold
    [...]
    all gates passed in chaos-verify:local

That run then refused to sign its own verdict, and the refusal belongs in this record:

    UNATTRIBUTABLE: the source tree changed while the gates ran.
      checksum 4284988695 -> 4053253308; path set 3458307932 unchanged, so file contents moved:
        docs/audit-followup-report.md
        docs/verification/never-true-cfg-2026-10-05.log
        docs/verification/todo-open-items.tsv
        scripts/cfg_lib.py
        scripts/ci/check-dead-cfg.py
        scripts/ci/test-check-dead-cfg.py
      nothing in this run can be attributed to a commit; re-run it with the tree at rest.

Six moved files, each one a file this batch was still writing while its own gates ran. A verdict
gathered while the tree moves cannot be attached to a commit, so the runner exits 1 and says why
rather than letting "all gates passed" be the last line anyone reads. The attributable form is a
clone checked out at a commit, where nothing can move underneath the checksum:

    $ git clone /home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code /var/tmp/full-<sha>
    $ cd /var/tmp/full-<sha> && git checkout --detach <sha>
    $ CHAOS_TREE_MANIFEST=/var/tmp/full-<sha>-manifest ./scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --full

The host sweep is the same label list with the build legs skipped. Its first run caught this batch
rather than the tree: `TODO status doc` failed, because the `TODO.md` row added for this work sits
above ten of the rows the export copies out and moves their line numbers, and the gate re-derives
`docs/verification/todo-open-items.tsv` from the file on disk instead of from intent. 37 gates
passed around it, which is the shape of the mistake -- one stale generated file, everything else
quiet:

    $ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh      # first run, export not yet refreshed
    PASS  toolchain matches the pin
    [...]
    FAIL  TODO status doc (exit 1)
    [...]
    PASS  secret scan
    gates exit=1

`python3 scripts/ci/classify-open-todos.py --write-export` is the refresh; both of its checks are
quiet afterwards, and the sweep re-run with nothing else editing the tree has no failing gate:

    $ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh      # resting tree, export refreshed
    == gates extracted from /home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code/scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
       host run: the ${bootstrap} prefix is dropped, build gates are skipped
       a skipped build gate plus a changed Rust file is a NOT COVERED failure, not a pass
       env: RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216 (mirrors the container's --env; set it to override)
    PASS  toolchain matches the pin
    [...]
    PASS  panic-site census
    PASS  dead cfg
    PASS  metric labels
    [...]
    PASS  secret scan
    SKIP  cargo check (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    SKIP  cargo clippy (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    SKIP  cargo test (build gate; --with-build runs it)
    SKIP  GUI protocol types (build gate; --with-build runs it)

    NOT COVERED  4 build gate(s) skipped while 1 changed file(s) are theirs to measure:
                  crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/agent/auth_method.rs
                  run: scripts/verify-gates.sh --with-build
    gates exit=1

    $ grep -c '^PASS' {SCRATCH}/b20/gates2.txt; grep -c '^FAIL' {SCRATCH}/b20/gates2.txt
    38
    0

The exit 1 there is the design and not a failure: a changed Rust file whose build gates were
skipped is reported as NOT COVERED rather than as a pass, which is the mechanism that sends a batch
to the container instead of letting it stop at "gates passed". Those build legs are what the
`--full` section above ran, and the `cargo test` leg is where this module's 20 tests are actually
executed.

== 9. What this does not cover

- `xai-grok-shell`'s own tests run in the Linux `cargo test --workspace` job
  (`.github/workflows/ci.yml:175`). The macOS and Windows legs compile `xai-grok-shell-terminal`
  and four other target-OS crates, not this one, so the revived tests are Linux-only in CI. That
  matches what they test: the module has no platform-specific path.
- The guard answers one question -- can this predicate ever hold -- and not the next one, whether
  the text behind it should be repaired or deleted. The fork parked this module three times
  because curating it looked like more work than silencing it; the gate does not do that work, it
  makes silencing it a build failure instead of a non-event.
- The fold is conservative in the direction that costs false positives nothing: any predicate the
  evaluator cannot decide is UNKNOWN and reported as fine. A `cfg(feature = "...")` naming a
  feature that no build in this repository turns on is therefore still invisible -- that question
  lives in `Cargo.toml`, not in the shape of the predicate, and no gate here answers it today.
  `check-load-bearing-features.py` answers a different feature question: whether a dependency
  feature still reaches a crate on every target.
- Nothing here repairs the two upstream syncs that silently discarded this module's newer tests.
  The sync procedure is a separate row in `TODO.md`.
